From: Ivan Lugo <ivan.lugo.dev@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 01:02:07 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] relax: const_drain makes an item in the unit that noted it

The owner's constant drain took the process-wide queue of what background
fronts found unevaluated (CONSTS_WANTED) and made every item in cur_unit():
the unit whose code the draining thread runs. That is the program's unit
between requests, but const_drain is also a Job request (prefront_slot), and
a Job is served inside any wait the owner pumps. A proc macro's call runs
on the owner inside the macro world's unit (pmhost::call -> call_jit_in sets
the thread's unit to it); a lazy compile in there waits on a type check
another thread runs, the pump serves a program front's const_drain, and the
program's DefIds / TyIds / FnKeys were read in the macro world's program.
The epoch check does not catch it: both units' collect epochs are equal.
On a warm start that builds the macro world beside the running program this
ended the process on the owner thread: a host SIGSEGV in Unit::vtable,
Unit::const_addr or Unit::promoted_addr (an id past the macro world's
tables), or an illegal instruction in constant-evaluation code (an id that
names another item there).

Each queued item now names the unit that noted it, and the drain makes it
there. An item whose unit is none the thread can name as alive (its own, the
runtime's, the booted program's, a live auxiliary unit) is made by no drain,
and retire_unit takes a retiring unit's items out of the queue with its other
address-keyed state. wanted_key holds the unit too, so equal ids of two
units are two items for the left-out builds.

tier_stuck_tests: the_drain_makes_an_item_in_the_unit_that_noted_it drains
while the owner's thread unit is another unit's.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
